digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(39,0,0) Currently, concepts that combine both low cost and high structural versatility for DNA-origami superstructures are missing (gap statement)."];
  "N10" [label="(12,0,0) DNA origami nanostructures have complete addressability for placing guest molecules with base-pair precision (a key advantage to preserve)."];
  "N11" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that structural DNA nanotechnology is widely used in synthetic biology and that DNA origami provides base-pair-precise addressability, therefore DNA origami is a strong candidate platform for modular, addressable superstructures."];
  "N12" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, a bottleneck in DNA-origami nanostructure design is the final size limit imposed by the scaffold length (e.g., 7249 bases for M13mp18)."];
  "N13" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that DNA origami is a strong candidate platform for modular, addressable superstructures and that scaffold length limits final DON size, therefore scaling to large superstructures requires strategies beyond single-scaffold size."];
  "N16" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, DNA origami slat assembly is limited to planar 2D structures with low assembly complexity (limits versatility/3D complexity)."];
  "N17" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, ssDNA-connector 3D superstructures require many unique connectors per connection, making assemblies prone to unwanted interactions and incomplete connections (scalability/robustness issue)."];
  "N18" [label="(38,0,0) Currently, many-different-DONs (heteromultimeric) superstructures provide control/addressability but require many distinct DONs and/or multistep assembly, increasing cost/effort and reducing yield."];
  "N19" [label="(34,0,0) Currently, self-complementary (homomultimeric) hierarchical assemblies are simple/cost-effective but lose structural control beyond a repetitive subunit (cannot control final structure)."];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that heteromultimeric superstructures retain control/addressability but are expensive/low-yield and that homomultimeric assemblies are cheap but lose control/addressability beyond the subunit, therefore a hybrid concept is needed that preserves control/addressability while avoiding many unique components and multistep assembly."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that the field lacks low-cost and high-versatility concepts and that a hybrid concept is needed to preserve control/addressability without many unique components or multistep assembly, therefore pursuing such a modular hybrid concept directly addresses the field gap."];
  "N28" [label="(44,0,0) Currently, the design engineers modularity into two different connection methods (a 2D/xy and a 3D/z mode)."];
  "N29" [label="(45,0,0) Currently, the first modularity method is an xy-direction connection strategy based on shape-matching connection sites."];
  "N30" [label="(49,0,0) Currently, the second modularity method is a three-strand connection system in the z-direction."];
  "N32" [label="(47,0,0) Currently, DNA origami typically used one fixed scaffold routing per structure (limits reconfigurability of connection-site layouts)."];
  "N33" [label="(46,0,0) Currently, a multitude of connection sites can be achieved by engineering a re-routable scaffold."];
  "N34" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that fixed scaffold routing limits reconfiguration and that a re-routable scaffold yields a multitude of connection sites, therefore re-routable routing is a mechanism to create many xy shape-matching connection configurations from one design paradigm shift."];
  "N35" [label="(48,0,0) Currently, using the re-routable-scaffold xy method, hundreds of moDON monomers with different shape-matching connection sites can be constructed."];
  "N36" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that the xy method can generate hundreds of distinct monomers/configurations and that the xy method is an xy-direction shape-matching connection strategy, therefore the xy modularity approach provides high combinatorial versatility in planar (xy) connectivity."];
  "N37" [label="(50,0,0) Currently, orthogonal binding sites with well-positioned, directional, optimally sized handles can circumvent three-strand-connection problems (lack of rigidity, poor stoichiometry control, too many unique strands)."];
  "N38" [label="(51,0,0) Currently, z-connections are engineered to be directional, non-branching, removable, rigid, orthogonal, low-duplex, and use only one DNA sequence per connection site."];
  "N39" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that engineered orthogonal directional handles can circumvent three-strand-connection problems and that z-connections are engineered to be orthogonal/rigid/directional with low sequence complexity per site, therefore z connections can be robust, controllable, and less prone to unwanted interactions than many-unique-connector schemes."];
  "N4" [label="(2,0,0) Modularity means systems are divided into subunits that can be independently addressed/changed without affecting the rest."];
  "N42" [label="(42,0,0) Currently, a large number of orthogonal connection sites allows increasing the number of monomers in one-pot assemblies and circumvents extraction/purification of intermediates used in traditional hierarchical assembly."];
  "N43" [label="(55,0,0) Currently, superstructures can be assembled in one-step reactions from monomers without purification/isolation of intermediates (unlike traditional hierarchical assembly)."];
  "N44" [label="(56,0,0) Currently, one-step assembly simplifies construction and increases overall yield."];
  "N45" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that orthogonal connection sites enable one-pot assembly without intermediate purification and that superstructures can be assembled in one-step from monomers without purification/isolation of intermediates, therefore the approach should improve practicality and yield relative to multi-step hierarchical assembly."];
  "N46" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that one-step assembly simplifies construction and increases yield and that one-pot assembly enabled by orthogonal sites should improve practicality and yield relative to multi-step hierarchical assembly, therefore one-pot modular assembly is a promising strategy to address the low-yield drawback of multistep hierarchical assembly."];
  "N47" [label="(57,0,0) Currently, xy- and z-connections are fully orthogonal, enabling parallel and selective assembly/disassembly of each connection via different orthogonal triggers."];
  "N48" [label="(58,0,0) Currently, site-specific addressability of each monomer in the superstructure is fully retained (demonstrated by placing Au nanoparticles at specific positions)."];
  "N5" [label="(4,0,0) Modularity enables low-effort and cost-effective assembly across domains (e.g., buildings, hardware, code, genetic code)."];
  "N51p" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, reusing the same component set across many designs typically reduces per-design synthesis cost and effort, while configurability increases the reachable design space."];
  "N53" [label="(41,0,0) Currently, moDON is a single modular DNA origami formed from one set of staples and can fold into tens of thousands of different connection configurations."];
  "N54" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given the moDON property of one-staple-set plus many configurations and the knowledge that reuse reduces cost while configurability increases design space, therefore moDON’s one-staple-set + many-configurations property is a plausible mechanism for low cost plus high versatility."];
  "N6" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that modularity creates independently addressable subunits and that modularity enables low-effort, cost-effective assembly across domains, therefore modular design is a plausible route to low-cost construction of complex systems."];
  "N60" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that moDON’s one-staple-set + many-configurations property is a plausible mechanism for low cost plus high versatility and that the modular connection design plausibly enables controlled assembly/disassembly while preserving precision, therefore the moDON concept plausibly satisfies the missing low-cost + high-versatility + controllable + addressable requirements."];
  "N9" [label="(8,0,0) Structural DNA nanotechnology is widely used as a construction approach in synthetic biology."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Research proposal: develop and demonstrate moDON, a single re-routable scaffold DNA origami monomer folded from one staple set, incorporating many orthogonal xy shape-matching sites and a one-sequence, rigid, directional three-strand z connector to enable cost-effective one-pot assembly and selective disassembly of fully addressable three-dimensional DNA-origami superstructures, including structures exceeding 1 GDa, while avoiding major cost, yield, and control drawbacks of conventional hierarchical methods.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A standardized DNA-origami building block that can be reconfigured through orthogonal xy and z connection modes can combine low component cost, high structural versatility, one-pot assembly, selective disassembly, and retained site-specific addressability."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The combination of fully orthogonal, selectively triggerable xy- and z-connections with retained site-specific addressability supports controlled assembly/disassembly while preserving addressable positioning within the superstructure."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Given demonstrated site-specific addressability and orthogonal selective triggers for xy- and z-connections, the modular connection design plausibly enables controlled assembly/disassembly while preserving functional precision."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) A DNA-origami-based route to large 3D superstructures is needed that is low-cost, versatile and controllable, suitable for one-pot/high-yield assembly, and retains addressability."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) The two modularity methods comprise an xy-direction shape-matching connection strategy and a z-direction three-strand connection system."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) Re-routable scaffold routing provides many xy shape-matching connection configurations, and the engineered z-connection supplies a directional, rigid, removable, orthogonal, low-sequence-complexity vertical connection mode."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) moDON plausibly satisfies the missing combined requirements for low-cost, high-versatility, controllable, addressable DNA-origami superstructures by combining one-staple-set configurability with controlled assembly/disassembly and retained addressability."];
  "R37" [label="(0,0,0) A design that engineers modularity into both xy shape-matching and z three-strand connection modes supports connectivity in planar and vertical directions."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) DNA origami slat assembly and ssDNA-connector 3D superstructures illustrate distinct limitations of existing approaches: slats are limited to planar, low-complexity 2D assemblies, whereas ssDNA-connector 3D assemblies require many unique connectors and are prone to unwanted interactions or incomplete connections."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because single-scaffold DNA origami is size-limited and some reported scaling approaches have specific cost, dimensionality, or connector-complexity drawbacks, scalable DNA-origami superstructures require strategies that go beyond single-scaffold size while avoiding those identified limitations."];
  "N4" -> "N6" [label="induction-case"];
  "N5" -> "N6" [label="induction-common"];
  "N9" -> "N11" [label="induction-case"];
